fact WorkItem(id: "WI-917", description: some(value: "AN AMBIGUOUS HEAD SEGMENT OF A DOTTED PATH IS REPORTED NOWHERE, and at a query pattern it is reported FALSELY. WI-907 made the whole-name ambiguity terminal and reported (load error at a reference, candidate-naming refusal at a query head / negation-committed goal). The DOTTED half is untouched: for `Sort.member` with two `Sort`s in scope, resolve_in_scope on the whole dotted string is NotFound, then resolve_dotted_in_kb stands BOTH rungs down under an ambiguous head (dotted_by_head needs Found; dotted_absolute is blocked by head_owns_path, whose doc calls the ambiguity 'a real finding with a real error to emit' — but nothing emits it), so the path falls to the WI-476 bare intern. DRIVEN, both halves: (1) REFERENCE — `rule r907(?x) :- Widget907.w907a(v: ?x)` with two wildcard-imported `Widget907`s LOADS CLEAN, no diagnostic, on a position that HAS an error channel. (2) QUERY — `anthill query 'SortInfo.si907a(v: ?x)'` under the same imports reports \"does not resolve to a known functor — no rule, fact, or declaration is in scope for it\", the exact false message WI-907 removed for the short-name case, one dot away. SECOND, SEPARATE GAP, also driven: a short ambiguous name in a query position that TOLERATES unresolvable names is silent — `push_choice(w907a(v: ?x), SortInfo(name: ?n))` answers 1 solution, exit 0, while pre-WI-907 that branch bound anthill.reflect.SortInfo and answered 4 rows; a data slot `w907a(v: SortInfo(name: ?n))` likewise. That silence is INHERITED from WI-863's deliberate non-descent into a bare disjunction / quantifier body / data slot, whose stated reason ('an undefined name there does not corrupt the correct answer') was written for an ABSENT name and has not been re-argued for an ambiguous one — decide whether it transfers. Both gaps raised by /code-review's altitude pass on the WI-907 diff and then DRIVEN with the built CLI. FIRST STEP: decide where the dotted-head ambiguity is REPORTED — head_owns_path is read by resolvers with a channel (remap_name_str_inner) and by ones without (resolve_name_in_kb), so the report cannot live in the rung; the ladder's existing split (VisibleOnly resolve, then an 'Any' diagnostic pass for positions with a channel — Loader::forbid_if_dotted_internal) is the shape that already solves this for 'internal', and WI-911 is lifting that pass off Loader. ACCEPTANCE: an ambiguous dotted head is reported as an ambiguity naming its candidates at a reference and at a query pattern; the WI-863 tolerance question is answered with a stated reason at undefined_query_goal_functors; docs/kernel-language.md 8.6's 'what is not yet reported' paragraph (added by WI-907) is narrowed to whatever remains; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-01T05:57:45Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-917", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED (commit 55fb7989). BOTH GAPS DRIVEN before and after, through the shipped CLI, as the ticket asked.\n\nTHE FIRST STEP THE TICKET ASKED FOR ('decide where the dotted-head ambiguity is REPORTED') RESOLVED TO 'NOWHERE NEW'. The ticket's own framing — head_owns_path is read by resolvers with a channel and without, so the report cannot live in the rung — was right about the rung and wrong about needing a new site: the answer is that the LADDER stops carrying Option<Symbol>. resolve_dotted_in_kb now returns ResolveResult and the contested head is returned BEFORE either rung is consulted, so every existing reporter — the three loader positions, the CLI's report_unknown_functor_name, the mount's ExtentRegError — got the ambiguity through the arm it ALREADY had for a contested whole name. No new diagnostic site, no lift off Loader, so WI-911 is untouched rather than needed first. The ticket named Loader::forbid_if_dotted_internal's split (VisibleOnly resolve, then an 'Any' diagnostic pass) as 'the shape that already solves this'; it is the shape for the INTERNAL half only, because a hidden hit is information the first read genuinely lacked, whereas vis never reaches the head.\n\nMADE ILLEGAL STATE UNREPRESENTABLE RATHER THAN CHECKED: the early return killed head_owns_path's Ambiguous arm, so the rungs now take Option<Symbol> and the state they used to guard against cannot reach them. wi751_ambiguous_head_is_not_resolved_past still passes and now ALSO reports — its doc named removed code and was corrected.\n\nGAP 2 ANSWERED 'DOES NOT TRANSFER', by WI-863's OWN reason: the tolerance holds because an ABSENT name's branch has no solutions to lose. A contested one's branch answers under either reading, so tolerating it DROPS solutions — the corruption the tolerance exists to avoid. Implemented as a SEPARATE walk (ambiguous_query_names, every node — goal or data, negated or not), not a flag on the existing one: the two questions have opposite descent rules for one reason, that walk asks what the SEARCH commits to and an ambiguity is a defect of the TEXT.\n\nTHREE FALSE OR WORTHLESS THINGS OF MINE, all caught by my own review pass rather than shipped:\n(1) A test I wrote as a DEFECT PIN — an_ambiguous_dotted_head_does_not_fall_through_to_the_implicit_tier — was green on BOTH sides. The tier is keyed on a name's LAST SEGMENT (kernel_vocab_qualified's rsplit), so it can never answer a dotted name at all. Deleted, and the same false claim removed from the comment in resolve_name_in_kb that had motivated it.\n(2) My first '4 rows -> 0 rows' measurement for gap 2 conflated the IMPLICIT TIER answering (WI-907's fall-through, no imports) with a CANDIDATE READING answering. On WI-907's fixture the contested SortInfo is a sort with no clauses, so nothing was actually dropped and the fixture could not show the defect. Rebuilt a fixture where every contested name answers under either reading (a rule in both namespaces; a fact whose DATA slot names a contested constructor), re-measured — one row under either import alone, 'no solutions' exit 0 under both — and corrected the prose at three sites.\n(3) A test-header claim quoted a query string I had never run. Measured the exact string against a temporarily reverted ladder before keeping it.\n\nPRE-FIX VERIFICATION IS THE CONTROL: 3 of the 6 core tests were confirmed to FAIL against a one-line revert of the ladder's Ambiguous arm, with the 2 both-sides controls still green — which is what proves the refusals are the ambiguity being caught and not the head-qualification rung having stopped working.\n\nREVIEW'S WIN WAS CONSOLIDATION, not deletion this time: after my change 'an answer ends the ladder' had FOUR spellings (two !matches!(.., NotFound), one Found|Ambiguous => true arm, one nested match). It is now ResolveResult::or_else / denotes, one home for the WI-907 rule that this ticket had to restate at every site. Three loader positions each spelled the dotted step out; that is resolve_dotted_reported once, because the ORDER is the policy.\n\nEFFICIENCY: neutral. The ladder returns 32 bytes instead of 8 on a path resolve_in_scope already pays for, and the Ambiguous Vec is now MOVED rather than built-and-dropped. The query-side walk is new but runs only over query patterns, orders the cheap ladder read ahead of the discrimination-tree backstop, and fires per undefined node only.\n\n/code-review NOT RUN — it is user-invocable only. The four /simplify angles were applied inline; that is not a substitute.\n\nFOLLOW-UP: WI-918 (the dotted message names the path but lists the HEAD's candidates — deliberate, since the full paths would name one that resolves to nothing, but the wording decision is unrecorded). WI-911 remains as it was: the mount still takes only the ladder's VisibleOnly half, and wi908's test doc was updated to say why its AMBIGUITY half no longer needs that lift.", at: "2026-08-01T05:57:47Z")
